#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179461 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3be2c44e-775f-53fa-a078-cff25eaea903" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "7cec344e-596a-51ce-87cc-d0e44dc48df4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7118" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$, where $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t) = t\\,\\mathbf{i} + t^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in\\left[0,1\\right]$." :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = t, \\qquad y(t) = t^2\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})j \\\\\n&= i + 2tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)(x^{2} + y)dx + xdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}(x^{2} + y)\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + x\\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}((x^{2} + y)\\frac{dx}{dt} + x\\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}(t^{2} + t^{2}) \\cdot 1 + t \\cdot 2tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}2t^{2} + 2t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}4t^{2}dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{1}t^{2}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179521 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/id #uuid "2b0b2d1f-df55-56cf-9ae9-ccef499e2352" :question/math-academy-id "e-9517" :question/problem "Construct a definite integral that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^3\\,\\mathrm{d}x + z\\,\\mathrm{d}y + y\\,\\mathrm{d}z$, where $C$ is a portion of the helix $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2t\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos t\\,\\mathbf{j} + \\sin t\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]$?" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) =2t, \\qquad y(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad z(t) = \\sin t\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j + \\frac{dz}{dt}k \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)k \\\\\n&= 2i - \\sin tj + \\cos tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)x^{3}dx + zdy + ydz &= ∫_{0}^{π}x^{3}\\frac{dx}{dt}dt + z\\frac{dy}{dt}dt + y\\frac{dz}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}(x^{3}\\frac{dx}{dt} + z\\frac{dy}{dt} + y\\frac{dz}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}(2t)^{3} \\cdot (2) + \\sin t \\cdot (-\\sin t) + \\cos t \\cdot (\\cos t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}16t^{3} - \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}16t^{3} + (\\cos^{2} t - \\sin^{2} t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}16t^{3} + \\cos 2tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179526 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2daf57e1-a02d-5e5f-b4a4-bb8ae9638a75" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "c18fbc4a-24d5-517d-9a3c-949a11787076" :question/math-academy-id "e-9528" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$, where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]$." :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad y(t) = 3\\sin t\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + 3\\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)ydx - xdy &= ∫_{0}^{π}y \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt}dt - x \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}(y \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} - x \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}3\\sin t \\cdot (-\\sin t) - (\\cos t) \\cdot 3\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π} - 3\\sin^{2} t - 3\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&=-3∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&=-3∫_{0}^{π}dt \\\\\n&=-3t \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&=-3π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd967b6-defd-518a-9713-edf26a3f6379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}3t^{2} + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177671} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5abaf6-94fd-5705-85f2-f0ee69c7b76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{3} - 2\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177673} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf71bf1-ca06-54ad-ab7e-527d975daca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{2} + 5\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177674} {:answer/id #uuid "7c34fd97-c8e2-5fac-abd6-06191a98e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}5\\ln t + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177675} {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b5b1f8-2205-55dd-8481-133a50f9b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336846}] :question/id #uuid "416f9a6e-1aee-5dd5-ad70-acf59db83c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-124871" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along $C$, $x(t)=1$, $y(t)=t$, and $z(t)=t^2$, so $x'(t)=0$, $y'(t)=1$, and $z'(t)=2t$. Substitute both the coordinates and their derivatives:\n\n$$\\int_C 2y\\,dx+\\ln x\\,dy+3z\\,dz\n=\\int_1^3\\left(2t\\cdot0+\\ln(1)\\cdot1+3t^2\\cdot2t\\right)\\,dt\n=\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the equivalent integral is $\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt$, and its value is $[\\tfrac32t^4]_1^3=120$.\n\nThe original source accepted $\\int_1^3 6t^2\\,dt$ and its worked solution changed $3t^2\\cdot2t$ into $6t^2$. That simplification loses a factor of $t$. The study choice replaces that exponent with $3$; all other original choices are retained."}]}
